      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules",
      "problem": "Given that $y=(x+1){e}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+1$ and $v(x)={e}^{9x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=9{e}^{9x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)({e}^{9x})+(x+1)(9{e}^{9x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{9x}+9(x+1){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(1+9(x+1)){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(9x+10){e}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
