    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-343332",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{x}$",
      "worked_solution": "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
