    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-343576",
      "knowledge_point_id": "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules",
      "problem": "If $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{x+2},$ find ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n${y}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})(x+2)-{e}^{3x}(1)}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+6-1)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+5)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of ${y}^{′}(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{(3(1)+5)({e}^{3(1)})}{((1)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{8e^3}{9}"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "\\frac{8e^3}{9}"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-343576",
        "kp_title": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules",
