    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-343546",
      "knowledge_point_id": "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules",
      "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{x}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and the denominator $v(x)=x.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-1)-0}{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{6}{\\pi}"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "\\frac{6}{\\pi}"
        }
      ],
