      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules",
      "problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(x+1))({x}^{2}+1)-((x+1)^{2})(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)-2x(x+1)^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(({x}^{2}+1)-x(x+1))}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{2}-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(1-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
