    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6100",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-185",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules",
      "problem": "If $f(x) = e^{-2x}\\tan({x^2})$, then what is $f'(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-184",
      "knowledge_point_id": "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules",
      "problem": "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2^x +1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t&= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t        \t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot  \\left( 2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{e^{3x}  \\left( 3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2}\n\t\t}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
